\tag{frap}
\title{From incidence to moves}

\p{To describe a puzzle move without committing to coordinates, we need an
incidence record that says which pieces and labels belong together. Faces and
incidence define an abstract polytope without choosing lengths or an embedding
\citet{Section 2}{schulte2017regular}. Regularity supplies shape symmetries;
the Rubik construction applies facet rotations locally to incidence positions
\citet{Definitions 5--6}{marchetti2025rubik}. The sections below first make
the shape visible in triangle and tetrahedron examples, then build this action
and calculate its tetrahedron and cube consequences.}

